            {
              "id": "2b1b0db5-4ddc-3ce9-b8dd-4d905938d1c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behaviors. This is a common vulnerability in C and C++ programming.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen()`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nthis_len = strlen(c_str());\n```\n\nConsider using:\n\n```cpp\nthis_len = strnlen(c_str(), MAX_LEN);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LEN` is the maximum length of the string that `c_str()` can return. This ensures that `strnlen()` will not read past `MAX_LEN` characters, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library in C++.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "42f55f84-39b8-3d67-abee-50799a2c0f71",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that can lead to a buffer overflow, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial-of-service, or information disclosure. In this case, the function `wcslen()` is used, which can lead to a buffer overflow if the string `str` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using functions that do not check for buffer overflows. Instead, use safer alternatives that include bounds checking. In this case, you can use `wcsnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to consider.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cwchar>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t max_chars = /* maximum number of characters that str can hold */;\nnum_chars = static_cast<int>(wcsnlen(str, max_chars));\n```\n\nIn this code, `wcsnlen()` is used instead of `wcslen()`. The `max_chars` parameter ensures that `wcsnlen()` does not read past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cwchar>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f69d7587-1412-3807-b8ea-ff35b4981585",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to, but not including, the terminating null character. If the string does not have a null character, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters one, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nchar *ansi = /* ... */;\nsize_t length;\n\nif (ansi != NULL) {\n    length = strlen(ansi);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `ansi` is not `NULL` before passing it to `strlen`. This prevents `strlen` from reading memory it should not have access to.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library to use the `strlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0fdc99d7-9174-3c5b-ab62-4a027584f7c6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other unexpected behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `CloneString(c_str, strlen(c_str))`, there is a potential vulnerability if `c_str` is not null-terminated. This can lead to buffer overflow and other related issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings.\n\nAlso, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to count. This can prevent buffer overflows by ensuring that `strnlen` does not read past the end of your buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t len = strnlen(c_str, MAX_LEN);\nCloneString(c_str, len);\n```\n\nIn this version, `strnlen` is used instead of `strlen`, and `MAX_LEN` is the maximum number of characters that `strnlen` will count. This prevents `strnlen` from reading past the end of the buffer if `c_str` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strnlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "12a19003-4bc6-34df-b45a-514b5bcbdeb8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsecured URL Vulnerability is not specific to C++ programming language, but it is a general security vulnerability that can occur in any programming language or system that deals with URLs. It refers to the situation where an application uses unsecured (non-HTTPS) URLs for transmitting sensitive data. This can lead to Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks where an attacker can intercept and possibly alter the communication between two parties without their knowledge.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use secure HTTPS URLs for transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS ensures that the data is encrypted and cannot be read by anyone other than the intended recipient, even if it is intercepted.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink you provided is a URL, not a piece of C++ code. However, if you were using this URL in a C++ program to transmit sensitive data, you should change it to use HTTPS:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string url = \"https://analogous.blogspot.com\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies would depend on the rest of your C++ code. If you're using a library to handle HTTP requests, you would need that library. For example, if you're using the `curl` library, you would need `libcurl`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "59a97074-7942-3442-804f-0eb731545b0c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function where the source and destination buffers may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions due to data flow between them. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source pointer is not null, which could also lead to a crash or undefined behavior. The destination buffer is a pointer with no known capacity, and the length argument is not derived from the destination's size, increasing the risk of buffer overflows or memory corruption. There is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can also lead to string handling errors. All these factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be remediated.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add a check before the copy operation to verify that the memory regions are distinct. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy. If overlap is possible and unavoidable, consider using a memory-safe function such as `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Also, ensure that the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy if required by your application logic.\n\n```c\nif (src != NULL && (dest + n <= src || src + n <= dest)) {\n    strncpy(dest, src, n);\n    dest[n - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination if appropriate\n} else {\n    // Handle error: overlapping regions or null pointer\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b72bbac0-7885-36bc-8dfe-4779ad410f49",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nUnsecured URL Vulnerability is not a vulnerability specific to the C++ programming language. It is a general web security issue that can occur in any programming language or framework. This vulnerability arises when an application uses unsecured (non-HTTPS) URLs to transmit sensitive data, such as login credentials, personal information, or other confidential data. This can allow attackers to intercept and read the data being transmitted, leading to potential data breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always use secure HTTPS URLs when transmitting sensitive data. HTTPS encrypts the data being transmitted, making it unreadable to anyone who might intercept it. Additionally, validate and sanitize all URLs and other user input to prevent other types of attacks, such as cross-site scripting (XSS) or SQL injection.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nSince this is not a C++ specific vulnerability, a source code fix recommendation is not applicable. However, in general, always use HTTPS URLs when transmitting sensitive data. For example, instead of:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string url = \"http://en.wikipedia.org\";\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string url = \"https://en.wikipedia.org\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nNo specific library dependencies are required for this example.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Transport Layer Protection Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Protection_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Unvalidated_Redirects_and_Forwards_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6d3d0e29-c936-33d7-a660-dc53d22e4ed3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the language. In this case, consider using a method that does not allow an attacker to control the environment variable.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a method that does not allow an attacker to control the environment variable. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* p = std::getenv(\"HCC_HOME\");\nif(p != nullptr) {\n    // Use the value of p\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6027282d-9f03-34b8-ab18-18098559fd2c",
