            {
              "id": "7e7092b3-a9ec-3165-b4a2-0664251f3e67",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5f0d53b2-8872-32a9-a6a8-a5e78608c366",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1f106b5a-ff39-3ac2-86fd-cec5be77d12d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to, but not including, the null-terminating character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to a buffer overflow or other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `!strlen(next)`, the vulnerability arises if `next` is not a null-terminated string. This could lead to unexpected behavior or even a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. This can be done by initializing strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character to the end of the string after it has been modified.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nif (next != NULL && next[0] != '\\0') {\n    // Your code here\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if `next` is not NULL and then check if the first character of `next` is not the null character. This ensures that `next` is a null-terminated string before we use it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb47916e-56a5-318a-9527-7d84505130cb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the `memmove` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the `count` parameter is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `memcpy_s` or `memmove_s` in this case, which are bounds-checked and will not cause buffer overflow. It is also important to validate and control all input data that is used as parameters to these functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `memmove_s`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nerrno_t error = memmove_s((char*)dst + dst_offset, sizeof(dst) - dst_offset, (char*)src + src_offset, count);\nif (error) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `string.h`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1eeaf63d-86df-3949-968c-218823e5d97b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions. These functions are susceptible to various types of attacks, such as collision attacks, preimage attacks, and second preimage attacks. In these attacks, an adversary can find two different inputs that hash to the same output, or find an input that hashes to a specific target output. This can lead to various security issues, such as unauthorized access, data corruption, and data leakage.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use strong, collision-resistant hash functions. These include SHA-256, SHA-3, and BLAKE2. Avoid using broken or weak hash functions, such as MD5 and SHA-1. Also, consider using a salt or a nonce to make preimage and second preimage attacks more difficult.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not seem to contain any hash algorithm or cryptographic function, so it's not possible to provide a specific source code fix recommendation. However, if a hash function is used elsewhere in the code, it should be replaced with a strong, collision-resistant hash function. For example, if MD5 is used, it can be replaced with SHA-256 as follows:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\n// ...\n\nunsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&digest); \n\nchar mdString[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH*2+1];\nfor (int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    sprintf(&mdString[i*2], \"%02x\", (unsigned int)digest[i]);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the SHA-256 function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "10fd7073-a407-304f-b37c-11a8a3820f0b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the `memmove` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if the `count` parameter is not properly validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as those provided by the C++ Standard Library. Also, always validate and sanitize input data, especially those used as parameters for these functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memmove` function with a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::copy` function from the C++ Standard Library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::copy(src, src + count, (char*)device + offset);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `cstring` for `memmove` function\n\nThe fixed code requires the following library:\n\n- `algorithm` for `std::copy` function\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8fdab178-4141-3cd4-be21-399398b64f5c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In this case, the `memmove()` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully.\n\nThe `memmove()` function copies `n` bytes from memory area `src` to memory area `dest`. The memory areas may overlap. If the size of the destination buffer (`dst`) is less than `count`, it can lead to buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Always validate input and carefully manage memory.\n- Use safer alternatives to these functions, such as `memcpy_s` or `memmove_s` in the case of `memmove()`.\n- If safer alternatives are not available, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `memmove_s`:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err = memmove_s(dst, sizeof(dst), (char*)device + offset, count);\nif (err) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2e235fc4-098a-3178-9c60-75dd6d60ae5f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of weak or broken cryptographic hash functions. These functions are susceptible to various types of attacks, including collision attacks, preimage attacks, and second preimage attacks. \n\nIn the context of MD2, it is a cryptographic hash function that is considered to be weak due to its vulnerability to collision attacks. This means that an attacker can generate two different inputs that hash to the same output, thereby breaking the integrity of the data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use stronger hash functions such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These functions are currently considered to be secure and resistant to known types of attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD2 with a stronger hash function. For example, if you are using OpenSSL library, you can replace `MD2()` with `SHA256()`.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\nunsigned char digest[MD2_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nMD2((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&digest); \n\n// Recommended fix\nunsigned char digest[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\nSHA256((unsigned char*)&data, sizeof(data), (unsigned char*)&digest);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe OpenSSL library is required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS126858"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "617d047f-d79d-317b-9096-41d09ded48fa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string can be controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For example, instead of `printf`, you can use `fprintf` or `snprintf` which are safer as they limit the number of characters to be printed, thus preventing buffer overflow attacks. Also, never allow user input to control format strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with `snprintf` function:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(hc::printf_buffer, sizeof(hc::printf_buffer), format_string, all...);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: This library is required for the `printf` function.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
